/-!
# The start-up's protocol constants: 𝖢 at 196616 and 𝖰 at 196680

Bootstrap's refine first asks `fetch` (Ω_Y, case ω₁₀ = 0) for the protocol constants (GP v0.8.0
Appendix B: ℰ(ℰ₈(𝖡_I), ℰ₈(𝖡_L), ℰ₈(𝖡_S), ℰ₂(𝖢), …, ℰ₂(𝖰), …), `JAM.Params.encode`). The prefix
(`PrefixC`, under `env.fetch.params = Params.full`) writes that reply at 206912, and the parameter
chain `BParams` decodes it into a struct at 4278058200 and copies fields into the service's globals.
In segment 174 (pc 56250 … 56316):

- 56250 `load_ind_u16 ω₁₁ (ω₈ + 114)`, 56253 `store_u64 ω₁₁ 196680`: the struct's 𝖰 (ℰ₂ at octet
  82 … 84 of the reply) becomes the 64-bit word at 196680;
- 56279 `store_u16 ω₃ 196616`: ω₃ holds 𝖢 (ℰ₂ at octet 24 … 26 of the reply), and it becomes the
  16-bit word at 196616.

The generated chain records only that these octets are written (`St177` frames them out). This file
re-proves segment 174 with the octets it writes (`steps174`, `seg174`), shows segments 175 and 176
keep them (`seg175`, `seg176`), and composes: `chain_consts` and `run_consts` are `BParams.chain`
and `BParams.run` with, in addition, 196616 … 196618 holding ℰ₂(𝖢) and 196680 … 196688 holding
ℰ₈(𝖰). `startup_consts` takes it from Y's state. The corollaries state the facts in the forms the
decoders assume (`Decode.Designate`'s 𝖢 at 196616, `Decode.Assign`'s word G at 196680 with
1 ≤ G mod 2³² ≤ 512), with the values read off `Params.full.encode`.

The segment proofs are copies of the generated `BParams.S174` … `S176` (`steps`/`run`) with the
stronger post-conditions; nothing in `BParams` is changed.
-/

